[83.349872, "o", "Quantum simulation of the Weyl equation with a trapped ion\r\nDe-Sheng Li,1, ∗ Chun-Wang Wu,1, † Lin-Ze He,1, ‡ Wei Wu,1, § and Ping-Xing Chen1, ¶\r\n1\r\nInterdisciplinary Center for Quantum Information,\r\nNational University of Defense Technology, Changsha 410073, P.R.China\r\n\r\narXiv:1712.05683v3 [quant-ph] 4 Apr 2019\r\n\r\nThe Weyl equation describes chiral massless relativistic particles, called Weyl fermions, which\r\nhave important relations to neutrinos. A direct observation of the dynamics of Weyl fermions in an\r\nexperiment is difficult to achieve. This study investigates a method of simulating the Weyl equation\r\nin 1+2-dimension by a single trapped ion. The predictions about a two-dimensional Zitterbewegung\r\nand an especially interesting phenomenon of Weyl fermions can be tested by the future trapped ion\r\nexperiment, which might enhance our understanding of neutrinos.\r\n\r\nINTRODUCTION\r\n\r\nThe Weyl equation can be derived from the Dirac equation as rest mass equals zero. Neutrinos have a nonzero\r\ntiny mass; hence, the Weyl equation can be used to approximately describe neutrinos. In the Standard Model\r\nof particle physics (SM), neutrinos are assumed to be\r\nmassless, and only left-handed neutrinos exist [1]. The\r\nfollowing remains an open question: why do left-handed\r\nneutrinos exist, but not right-handed neutrinos? Neutrinos do not have an electric charge, and are difficult\r\nto detect. Moreover, its properties are still not exactly\r\nclear. An analog quantum simulation [2–6] of the Weyl\r\nequation in a trapped ion system might give us a new perspective to understand neutrinos and the electro-weak\r\ninteraction. Analog quantum simulators aim at using\r\ncontrollable systems to simulate systems that are hard\r\nto access in an experiment. Some examples of the analog quantum simulation are as follows: a black hole is\r\nsimulated in Bose–Einstein condensation by sonic gravitation correspondence [7]; the Klein paradox is simulated using two ions [8]; the Majorana equation and unphysical operations are simulated using a trapped ion\r\n[9]; and quantum field theory is simulated using trapped\r\nions[10, 11]. The Zitterbewegung phenomenon, which is\r\na trembling motion for quantum-relativistic particles, including Weyl fermions, has been widely discussed in the\r\nrecent years[12–17]. For example, the theoretical proposal and the experimental result of the quantum simulation of the Dirac equation and Zitterbewegung have\r\nbeen provided by [12, 13]. The neutrino’s Zitterbewegung\r\nhas been discussed by [14].\r\nWe present herein a method of simulating the 1 +\r\n2-dimensional Weyl equation using the analog quantum simulator. The two-dimension Zitterbewegung of\r\nWeyl fermions can be tested by the trapped ion experiment. Another interesting phenomenon that can\r\nbe tested through the experiment is the axisymmetric\r\nWeyl fermion initial state that will evolve into a nonaxisymmetric state. We show that the Weyl equation\r\nevolution can be simulated in a single trapped ion using\r\nred- and blue-sideband (bichromatic) laser beams from\r\nthe x- and y-directions, respectively. The initial state\r\n\r\nis prepared by Doppler cooling, sideband cooling, and\r\ndisplacement Hamiltonian. The average momentum of\r\nthe simulated particle is adjusted by the displacement\r\nHamiltonian. We can use the wave function reconstruction method [13] to obtain the average position and possibility distribution at a certain time t by measuring the\r\nexcited state’s population.\r\n\r\nEFFECTIVE HAMILTONIAN, INITIAL STATE\r\nPREPARATION, AND EVOLUTION\r\n\r\nThe Weyl equation is written as follows:\r\ni~\r\n\r\n∂ΨL\r\n= −c(σx p̂x + σy p̂y + σz p̂z )ΨL ,\r\n∂t\r\n\r\n(1)\r\n\r\nwhere σi (i = x, y, z) are Pauli matrices; c is the speed\r\nof light; and ΨL is the state of the left-handed twocomponent Weyl fermions.\r\nWe consider an ion trapped in the linear Paul trap to\r\nsimulate the Weyl equation. For simplicity and to not\r\nlose generality, we choose the 40 Ca+ -ion as an example.\r\nAfter Doppler cooling and sideband cooling, the trap potential of the ion is treated as a harmonic potential, and\r\nthe"]
[83.349946, "o", " external state of the ion is treated as a quantum harmonic oscillator"]
[83.372414, "o", ". The external state of the ion is quantum\r\nstate and determined by the number of phonons. Each\r\nphonon’s energy is ~ω (Fig. 1). We choose two internal states of the 40 Ca+ -ion as |0i, |1i and |0i = S1/2 ,\r\n|1i = D5/2 . The 729 nm, 854 nm and 866 nm laser\r\nbeams are used to initialize the internal state of 40 Ca+\r\ninto |0i. The 729 nm laser beam can pump |0i into |1i\r\nand realize any possible internal state\r\nα|0i + β|1i,\r\n\r\n(2)\r\n\r\n"]
[83.38776, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@8df1cfe16dec:~/papers/all# "]
[83.8245, "i", "pdftotext 2504.10415v2.pdf - | head -80\n"]
[83.824773, "o", "pdftotext 2504.10415v2.pdf - | head -80\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[83.869826, "o", "LLM-SRBench: A New Benchmark for Scientific Equation Discovery with"]
[83.870087, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.870175, "o", "Large Language Models"]
[83.87025, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.870314, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.870386, "o", "Parshin Shojaee∗ 1 Ngoc-Hieu Nguyen∗ 2 Kazem Meidani 3 4 Amir Barati Farimani 3"]
[83.870491, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.870559, "o", "Khoa D Doan 2 Chandan K Reddy 1"]
[83.870633, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.870704, "o", "Website: https://github.com/deep-symbolic-mathematics/llm-srbench"]
[83.870792, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.870856, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.87093, "o", "arXiv:2504.10415v2 [cs.CL] 7 Jun 2025"]
[83.871012, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.871079, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.871138, "o", "Abstract"]
[83.871211, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.871278, "o", "Scientific equation discovery has long been a"]
[83.871349, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.871408, "o", "cornerstone of scientific progress, enabling the"]
[83.871482, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.87155, "o", "derivation of laws governing natural phenomena."]
[83.871636, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.871712, "o", "Recently, Large Language Models (LLMs) have"]
[83.871797, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.871872, "o", "gained interest for this task due to their potential to leverage embedded scientific knowledge"]
[83.871973, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.872043, "o", "for hypothesis generation. However, it is difficult to assess the true discovery capabilities of"]
[83.872128, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.872209, "o", "these methods because existing benchmarks often use well-known equations. This makes them"]
[83.872298, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.872371, "o", "vulnerable to memorization by LLMs and results"]
[83.872451, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.872528, "o", "in inflated performance metrics that do not reflect genuine discovery. In this paper, we introduce LLM-SRBench, a comprehensive benchmark with 239 challenging problems across four"]
[83.872627, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.872696, "o", "scientific domains specifically designed to evaluate LLM-based scientific equation discovery"]
[83.872779, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.872852, "o", "methods while preventing trivial memorization."]
[83.872941, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.873022, "o", "Our benchmark comprises two main categories:"]
[83.873109, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.873185, "o", "LSR-Transform, which transforms common physical models into less common mathematical representations to test reasoning beyond memorized"]
[83.873282, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.873354, "o", "forms, and LSR-Synth, which introduces synthetic, discovery-driven problems requiring datadriven reasoning. Through extensive evaluation"]
[83.873445, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.87352, "o", "of several state-of-the-art methods, using both"]
[83.873627, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.873722, "o", "open and closed LLMs, we find that the bestperforming system so far achieves only 31.5%"]
[83.873808, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.873884, "o", "symbolic accuracy. These findings highlight the"]
[83.873976, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.874052, "o", "challenges of scientific equation discovery, positioning LLM-SRBench as a valuable resource for"]
[83.874142, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.874216, "o", "future research."]
[83.87429, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.874365, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.874434, "o", "Figure 1. Error analysis comparing simple LLM sampling (Llama3.1-8B) on 100 Feynman problems versus LLM-SRBench datasets"]
[83.874518, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.874589, "o", "(LSR-Transform and LSR-Synth). The sharp drops in numeric"]
[83.874674, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.87478, "o", "error curves and considerably lower symbolic error for Feynman"]
[83.874852, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.87494, "o", "problems suggest memorization ra"]
[83.875025, "o", "ther than gradual discovery."]
[83.875092, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.875158, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.875225, "o", "1. Introduction"]
[83.875296, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.875374, "o", "Equation discovery, the process of uncovering symbolic"]
[83.875444, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.875521, "o", "mathematical expressions from observational data, has been"]
[83.875589, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.875664, "o", "a cornerstone of scientific advancement. This task, also"]
[83.875731, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.875823, "o", "known as symbolic regression (SR), goes beyond mere datadriven predictive modeling by seeking interpretable mathematical relations that reveal the underlying mechanisms"]
[83.875887, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.875984, "o", "of natural phenomena. When scientists derive mathematical equations from empirical data, they gain more than just"]
[83.876053, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.876133, "o", "predictive power – they obtain insights into fundamental"]
[83.876205, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.876279, "o", "physical principles, enable extrapolation beyond observed"]
[83.876341, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.876416, "o", "data, and facilitate knowledge transfer across scientific domains (Langley, 1981; Schmidt & Lipson, 2009)."]
[83.876476, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.876551, "o", "Standard approaches to equation discovery have primarily relied on genetic programming (GP) and evolutionary"]
[83.876617, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.876689, "o", "algorithms (Cranmer, 2023; La Cava et al., 2021), which"]
[83.876748, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.876814, "o", "represent mathematical expressions as trees and navigate the"]
[83.87687, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.87695, "o", "vast space of possible equations through evolutionary search"]
[83.87701, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.877077, "o", "techniques. However, these methods face two fundamental"]
[83.877139, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.877216, "o", "challenges. First, the NP-hard nature of equation discovery (Virgolin & Pissis, 2022) makes their random mutation"]
[83.877278, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.877344, "o", "and crossover operations computationally prohibitive across"]
[83.877402, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.877461, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.877523, "o", "*"]
[83.877581, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.879527, "o", "\r\n"]
[83.879592, "o", "Equal contribution 1 Virginia Tech 2 VinUniversity 3 Carnegie\r\nMellon University 4 Capital One. Correspondence to: Parshin\r\nShojaee <parshinshojaee@vt.edu>.\r\nProceedings of the 42 nd International Conference on Machine\r\nLearning, Vancouver, Canada. PMLR 267, 2025. Copyright 2025\r\nby the author(s).\r\n\r\n1\r\n\r\n\fLLM-SRBench: A New Benchmark for Scientific Equation Discovery with Large Language Models\r\n\r\n- Discover the mathematical equation/law\r\nthat describes [output variable] based on\r\ngiven [input features].\r\n- Use domain-specific knowledge of [the\r\nscientific field] and provided data samples\r\nto find an equation that is scientifically valid\r\nand fits the data well.\r\n\r\nLLM internal scientific\r\nknowledge\r\nReasoning and planning\r\n\r\nFind an equation in the field of classical\r\nmechanics that describes the mass ( 𝑚 ) needed to\r\nstore energy in an oscillating system, given\r\n"]
[83.887391, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@8df1cfe16dec:~/papers/all# "]
[97.250008, "i", "cd ..\n"]
[97.250243, "o", "cd ..\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[97.250382, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[97.250417, "o", "root@8df1cfe16dec:~/papers# "]
[97.695036, "i", "cat > classify_papers.py << 'EOF'\nimport os\nimport subprocess\nimport shutil\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n# Define keywords for each category\nkeywords = {\n    'LLM': ['language model', 'llm', 'transformer', 'bert', 'gpt', 'attention', 'nlp', 'natural language', 'embedding', 'token', 'large language model'],\n    'trapped_ion_and_qc': ['trapped ion', 'quantum computing', 'qubit', 'quantum', 'ion trap', 'phonon', 'quantum circuit', 'quantum gate'],\n    'black_hole': ['black hole', 'schwarzschild', 'einstein', 'gravity', 'general relativity', 'spacetime', 'horizon', 'weyl', 'gravitational'],\n    'DNA': ['dna', 'gene', 'protein', 'sequence', 'nucleotide', 'helix', 'molecular', 'chromosome', 'base pair', 'genetic'],\n    'music_history': ['music', 'composer', 'symphony', 'classical', 'harmony', 'melody', 'musical', 'concert', 'philharmonic']\n}\n\nsource_dir = 'papers/all'\ntarget_dirs = {\n    'LLM': 'papers/LLM',\n    'trapped_ion_and_qc': 'papers/trapped_ion_and_qc',\n    'black_hole': 'papers/black_hole',\n    'DNA': 'papers/DNA',\n    'music_history': 'papers/music_history'\n}\n\ndef extract_text_from_pdf(pdf_path):\n    \"\"\"Extract text from PDF using pdftotext\"\"\"\n    try:\n        result = subprocess.run(['pdftotext', pdf_path, '-'], \n                              capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)\n        return result.stdout.lower()\n    except:\n        return \"\"\n\ndef classify_paper(pdf_path):\n    \"\"\"Classify a paper based on keyword matching\"\"\"\n    text = extract_text_from_pdf(pdf_path)\n    \n    if not text:\n        return 'music_history'  # Default category\n    \n    scores = {}\n    for category, kw_list in keywords.items():\n        score = sum(text.count(kw) for kw in kw_list)\n        scores[category] = score\n    \n    # Return category with highest score\n    best_category = max(scores, key=scores.get)\n    return best_category if scores[best_category] > 0 else 'music_history'\n\ndef main():\n    # Get all PDF files\n    pdf_files = sorted([f for f in os.listdir(source_dir) if f.endswith('.pdf')])\n    \n    print(f\"Found {len(pdf_files)} PDF files\")\n    \n    classified = {cat: [] for cat in target_dirs.keys()}\n    \n    for i, pdf_file in enumerate(pdf_files):\n        pdf_path = os.path.join(source_dir, pdf_file)\n        print(f\"[{i+1}/{len(pdf_files)}] Classifying {pdf_file}...\", end=' ')\n        \n        category = classify_paper(pdf_path)\n        classified[category].append(pdf_file)\n        \n        # Move file to appropriate folder\n        target_path = os.path.join(target_dirs[category], pdf_file)\n        shutil.move(pdf_path, target_path)\n        print(f\"-> {category}\")\n    \n    # Print summary\n    print(\"\\n=== Classification Summary ===\")\n    for category, files in classified.items():\n        print(f\"{category}: {len(files)} files\")\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\nEOF\n"]
